The non-obvious fidelity points:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mcompute_hash[0m observes raw heap addresses.** Its result is not a constant — it varies with heap history (I measured 110, 120, and 220 for different call sequences, even at the same [38;5;10mblock_size[0m). To match, the Rust routes every allocation through libc [38;5;10mmalloc[0m/[38;5;10mcalloc[0m/[38;5;10mfree[0m with identical sizes and ordering, and the crate performs no Rust heap allocation at all, so the process heap evolves the same way. Reimplementing this with Rust's allocator would have silently diverged.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10m(param1 % 10) + 5[0m is computed in [38;5;10mint[0m, then widened to [38;5;10msize_t[0m.** For [38;5;10mparam1 % 10 < -5[0m this becomes a huge unsigned count, [38;5;10mcalloc[0m fails, and [38;5;10mbetagamma[0m returns [38;5;10m-1[0m. Preserved via [38;5;10mas isize as usize[0m — e.g. [38;5;10mbetagamma(-7,5,9,-3) == -1[0m and [38;5;10mbetagamma(-2147483648,1,1,1) == -1[0m.[0m[0m
- **Signed overflow wraps.** All arithmetic uses explicit [38;5;10mwrapping_*[0m; [38;5;10mbetagamma(2147483647,1,1,1) == -2147483162[0m matches.[0m[0m
- **Preserved as-is, not fixed:** the unbounded [38;5;10mstrcpy[0m into [38;5;10mchar name[32][0m (calls libc [38;5;10mstrcpy[0m directly), the [38;5;10mmem1->data > NULL[0m pointer-vs-[38;5;10mNULL[0m comparison that gcc warns about, the missing null checks in [38;5;10mcompute_hash[0m, the dead [38;5;10mtemp_name[0m copy, and the uninitialized trailing padding of the returned [38;5;10mDataBlock[0m (via [38;5;10mMaybeUninit[0m).[0m[0m
